<section xml:id="sec-high-performance-mathematical-computing">
<title>High-Performance Mathematical Computing</title>

<introduction>
<remark>
<title>Prerequisites</title>
<p>Scientific computing, numerical linear algebra, numerical differential
equations, Monte Carlo methods, sparse matrices, algorithmic complexity,
parallel computing concepts, and basic computer architecture.</p>
</remark>

<remark>
<title>Learning Objectives</title>
<p>By the end of this section, the reader should be able to:</p>

<p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>explain the purpose of high-performance mathematical computing;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>distinguish algorithmic, implementation, and hardware
          performance;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand computational throughput;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>recognize floating-point operation counts;</p>
</li>
<li>
<p>understand memory bandwidth limitations;</p>
</li>
<li>
